\import{macros}
\import{lean-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{Methods and search}

  \p{Once the constraints are known, a solver still needs a reasoned way to
  choose the next move. A solving method organizes a sequence of smaller
  goals: at each stage, some information has been fixed and some remains
  free. The useful mathematical question is which moves accomplish the next
  goal while preserving what has already been achieved. The stabilizer of the
  recorded data, as in the general construction
  \leanref{MulAction.stabilizer}, gives language to that requirement;
  successive stages suggest a chain of subgroups. Conjugation relocates the
  effect of a known sequence; commutators can help isolate effects, whose
  actual support must be checked in each example. Salkinder uses
  conjugates and commutators in the cube setting
  \citet{Sections 2 and 3.2}{salkinder2021nxnxn}.}

  \p{Explaining why a method works and judging how well it serves a player
  are related but different tasks. A complete method needs an argument
  that its stages cover the remaining cases and terminate at a solved
  state. Practical use also depends on recognition, memorization, and ease
  of execution. Computational search raises further questions about time,
  memory, and solution length.}

  \p{Cayley graphs describe move sequences as paths between group
  elements \citet{Section 4, Definition 4}{pan2021fourier}; actions on
  partial information lead to Schreier graphs. The generic labeled-edge
  construction is \leanref{Quiver.SchreierGraph}: a move labels an edge
  from a state to its image. For the left action of a group on itself,
  this is the Cayley viewpoint. These declarations supply stabilizers
  and action graphs; they do not establish a puzzle-solving method.
  Such descriptions become useful when comparing reduction
  strategies or searching for shorter sequences. Any length comparison
  must specify the allowed moves and their costs. The goal is to connect
  a method's observable effect with its mathematical explanation.
  [Scrambling and search functions](frap-0015) describe a related use
  of functions on the move group.}
